 ("F1", "Background", "Chapter 12 of the 3rd edition, Designing and Deploying Command Line Programs, is new in this edition and teaches how to run Python programs outside the code editor: the vocabulary of program, script, command, shell script, application, app and web app; the terminal, with the cd, pwd, dir and ls commands, running programs and opening files by name; the PATH environment variable, how Windows, macOS and Linux search it, how to add a Scripts folder to it, and the which and where commands; virtual environments made with python -m venv .venv, activated, deactivated and kept away from the system Python; pip run as python -m pip to install, list, upgrade, pin and uninstall packages from PyPI, and the automateboringstuff3 package; the built-in values __file__, sys.executable, sys.version, sys.version_info, os.name and sys.platform and the ModuleNotFoundError check; text-based program design - short command names, command line arguments in sys.argv with argparse left to its documentation, clipboard input and output with pyperclip, colourful text with Bext, clearing the terminal with os.system, sound with playsound3 and verbose and quiet modes; the Snowstorm animation; PyMsgBox's alert, confirm, prompt and password; deployment with a batch file on Windows, a .command file on macOS and a shell script with a .desktop entry on Ubuntu; the ccwd and Clipboard Recorder programs; PyInstaller's --onefile build; and it closes with a summary, 6 practice questions and one practice program.", ["P01"]),
